---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import InstanceNote from "../components/api/InstanceNote.astro";
import OptionsTable from "../components/api/OptionsTable.astro";
import PropertiesTable from "../components/api/PropertiesTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "introduction", label: "Introduction", heading: false },
  { id: "initialization", label: "Initialization" },
  { id: "display", label: "Display" },
  { id: "options", label: "Options" },
  { id: "internationalization", label: "Internationalization" },
  { id: "twelve-hour", label: "12-hour and 24-hour" },
  { id: "seconds", label: "Seconds" },
  { id: "time-range", label: "Time range" },
  { id: "methods", label: "Methods" },
  { id: "properties", label: "Properties" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="timepicker" sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.introduction}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          Add <code>time-picker</code> to a text input. <code>AutoInit()</code> starts every
          <code>.time-picker</code> except those marked <code>no-autoinit</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The clock is inline, not a modal. The picker appends it to the input's parent, and it stays
          visible. There is no <code>openByDefault</code> flag.
        </p>
        <div class="field mb-3">
          <input type="text" class="time-picker" id="timepicker-intro">
          <label for="timepicker-intro">Lunchtime</label>
        </div>
<Code code={`
<div class="field">
  <input type="text" class="time-picker" id="lunchtime">
  <label for="lunchtime">Lunchtime</label>
</div>
`} />
        <p>
          Wrap the input in its own <code>.field</code> (or another small parent). The
          picker appends the clock inside that parent.
        </p>
        <p>
          To pick a time, click an hour on the dial, then a minute. With the default
          <code>autoSubmit: true</code>, choosing the minute writes the input
          (<code>HH:MM AM</code> or <code>HH:MM PM</code>).
        </p>
        <p>
          You can also type in the digital
          fields. Enter in any of them writes the typed time.
          Digital edits use native input events, including paste. When focus leaves a field
          after an invalid edit, the field restores the last valid value. An empty field
          takes the current hour, minute or second and leaves the selected AM/PM period as it is.
        </p>
        <p>Click the host input or press Enter in it to reload its current value into the clock. Changing the active clock field, or editing its value, cancels an older delayed clock update.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section {...S.initialization}>
        <p>
          The IIFE bundle exposes <code>Expressive.Timepicker</code>.
          Call <code>init</code> yourself when you need options other than the defaults,
          or let <code>Expressive.AutoInit()</code> start every <code>.time-picker</code>.
          Unlike Datepicker, the default options already show the clock.
        </p>
<Code code={`
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
  const elems = document.querySelectorAll('.time-picker');
  const instances = Expressive.Timepicker.init(elems, {
    // specify options here
  });
});
`} />
        <p>You can also pass per-instance options through AutoInit:</p>
<Code code={`
Expressive.AutoInit(document.body, {
  Timepicker: { twelveHour: false }
});
`} />
  </Section>

  <Section {...S.display}>
        <p>
          By default the clock is a <code>.timepicker-container</code> in the page.
          <code>displayPlugin: 'modal'</code> puts it in a dialog instead. There is no
          <code>autoClose</code> option.
        </p>
        <p>
          To render the clock into a specific element instead of the
          input's parent, pass a selector or a DOM node as <code>container</code>.
        </p>
        <h5 class="light">Modal dialog</h5>
        <p>
          Set <code>displayPlugin: 'modal'</code> to open the clock in a modal
          <code>&lt;dialog class="display-modal"&gt;</code>, drawn as a basic
          dialog over a scrim. It opens when the user clicks the input or confirms it with
          Enter. The dialog takes the input's label as its name, and focus moves
          to the hour field.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ok, or Enter in a field, writes the time.
          Cancel, Escape or a press on the scrim closes the dialog without changing the
          input and calls <code>onCancel</code>. Focus then goes back to the
          input. A modal clock always shows Cancel and Ok, so it sets
          <code>autoSubmit: false</code> for you.
        </p>
<Code code={`
Expressive.Timepicker.init(elem, {
  displayPlugin: 'modal'
});
`} />
        <p>
          In a selective Sass build, add <code>"dialog"</code> and
          <code>"modal-display"</code> to <code>$components</code>.
        </p>
        <h5 class="light">Docked popover</h5>
        <p>
          Set <code>displayPlugin: 'docked'</code> to wrap the clock in a
          <code>.display-docked</code> popover. The popover appears when the user clicks the input or
          confirms it with Enter, and hides when they click outside.
        </p>
<Code code={`
Expressive.Timepicker.init(elem, {
  displayPlugin: 'docked'
});
`} />
        <p>
          The popover stays inside the input's parent and accounts for the parent's
          positioning and scroll offsets. You can use positioned wrappers
          such as <code>.field</code> directly.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>displayPluginOptions</code> is optional and takes <code>margin</code> (default
          <code>5</code>), <code>transition</code> (<code>10</code>),
          <code>duration</code> (<code>250</code>), and <code>align</code>
          (<code>'left'</code>).
        </p>
  </Section>

  <Section {...S.options}>
        <OptionsTable>
            <tr>
              <td><code>duration</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td><code>350</code></td>
              <td>Transition between the hours and minutes views, in milliseconds.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>container</code></td>
              <td>Element or String</td>
              <td><code>null</code></td>
              <td>Element or selector to render the clock into. When omitted, the picker appends the clock to the input's parent.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>showClearBtn</code></td>
              <td>Boolean</td>
              <td><code>false</code></td>
              <td>Show a Clear button in the footer.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>autoSubmit</code></td>
              <td>Boolean</td>
              <td><code>true</code></td>
              <td>If true, choosing a minute writes the input. If false, Ok and Cancel buttons are added.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>defaultTime</code></td>
              <td>String</td>
              <td><code>'now'</code></td>
              <td>Initial time. <code>'now'</code> or a <code>'HH:MM'</code> or <code>'HH:MM:SS'</code> string (optionally with <code>AM</code>/<code>PM</code>).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>fromNow</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td><code>0</code></td>
              <td>Millisecond offset added to <code>'now'</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>i18n</code></td>
              <td>Object</td>
              <td>See below</td>
              <td>Labels for Cancel, Clear, Ok, Hours, Minutes and Seconds.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>twelveHour</code></td>
              <td>Boolean</td>
              <td><code>true</code>, or the language's</td>
              <td>If true, use a 12-hour clock with AM/PM. If false, use 24-hour hours on two rings. With a <code>lang</code> on or around the input, it defaults to the clock that language uses.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>showSeconds</code></td>
              <td>Boolean</td>
              <td><code>false</code></td>
              <td>Add a seconds field after the minutes and write <code>HH:MM:SS</code>. The dial still sets hours and minutes only.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>minTime</code></td>
              <td>String</td>
              <td><code>null</code></td>
              <td>The earliest time a person can choose, as 24-hour <code>HH:MM</code> or <code>HH:MM:SS</code>. Without <code>showSeconds</code> it rounds up to a whole minute.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>maxTime</code></td>
              <td>String</td>
              <td><code>null</code></td>
              <td>The latest time a person can choose, as 24-hour <code>HH:MM</code> or <code>HH:MM:SS</code>. Without <code>showSeconds</code> it rounds down to a whole minute.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>vibrate</code></td>
              <td>Boolean</td>
              <td><code>true</code></td>
              <td>Vibrate the device when the clock hand changes value.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>onSelect</code></td>
              <td>Function</td>
              <td><code>null</code></td>
              <td>Called when a time is chosen on the dial. Receives <code>(hour, minute)</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>onInputInteraction</code></td>
              <td>Function</td>
              <td><code>null</code></td>
              <td>Called when the input is clicked or confirmed with Enter.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>onDone</code></td>
              <td>Function</td>
              <td><code>null</code></td>
              <td>Called once when Ok is activated by click, Enter or Space. The button is created when <code>autoSubmit</code> is false.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>onCancel</code></td>
              <td>Function</td>
              <td><code>null</code></td>
              <td>Called once when Cancel is activated by click, Enter or Space. The button is created when <code>autoSubmit</code> is false.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>displayPlugin</code></td>
              <td>String</td>
              <td><code>null</code></td>
              <td>Set to <code>'docked'</code> for a click-to-open popover, or <code>'modal'</code> for a dialog with Cancel and Ok.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>displayPluginOptions</code></td>
              <td>Object</td>
              <td><code>null</code></td>
              <td>Options for the docked plugin: <code>margin</code>, <code>transition</code>, <code>duration</code>, <code>align</code>. The modal plugin takes none.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>dialRadius</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td><code>135</code></td>
              <td>Radius of the dial, in pixels. The clock face is drawn at twice this size inside <code>.timepicker-plate</code>, which CSS fixes at 260px. Change the plate's <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> in step with this option.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>outerRadius</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td><code>105</code></td>
              <td>Distance from the center to the minutes and the outer hour ring, in pixels.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>innerRadius</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td><code>70</code></td>
              <td>Distance from the center to the inner hour ring of a 24-hour clock, in pixels.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>tickRadius</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td><code>20</code></td>
              <td>Radius of the circle that marks the selected value, in pixels. Each <code>.timepicker-tick</code> is 40px in CSS, twice the default, so resize the ticks in step with this option.</td>
            </tr>
        </OptionsTable>
  </Section>

  <Section {...S.internationalization}>
        <p>Pass a partial <code>i18n</code> object. Missing keys keep the English defaults. The <code>hours</code>, <code>minutes</code> and <code>seconds</code> keys give the inputs their accessible names.</p>
        <p>The AM/PM buttons expose their selected state and support Enter and Space. The hour, minute and second fields are spin buttons. Up and Down step them by one, and Page Up and Page Down step by five, wrapping round. The hand follows the hour and minute. Hours step round the day, so in 12-hour time, passing 12 switches AM and PM. Up from 11 AM comes 12 PM. Tab moves between the fields, the AM/PM buttons and the footer.</p>
        <p>The dial itself takes pointer input only. The spin buttons set the same values from the keyboard and for a screen reader, so a focusable dial would only add a second Tab stop for them.</p>
        <table class="striped">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Key</th>
              <th>Default</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>cancel</code></td>
              <td><code>'Cancel'</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>clear</code></td>
              <td><code>'Clear'</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>done</code></td>
              <td><code>'Ok'</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr><td><code>hours</code></td><td><code>'Hours'</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>minutes</code></td><td><code>'Minutes'</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>seconds</code></td><td><code>'Seconds'</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
  </Section>

  <Section {...S["twelve-hour"]}>
        <p>
          Set <code>twelveHour: false</code> for a 24-hour dial, with hours
          1 to 12 on the inner ring and 13 to 00 on the outer ring. The written value is then
          <code>HH:MM</code> with no meridian. The intro clock above is
          12-hour with AM/PM.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>twelveHour</code> defaults to <code>true</code>. With a <code>lang</code>
          on or around the input, it defaults to the clock that language uses. That is 24-hour for
          <code>de</code> or <code>fr</code>, and 12-hour for <code>en-US</code>.
        </p>
        <div class="field mb-3">
          <input type="text" class="time-picker no-autoinit" id="timepicker-24">
          <label for="timepicker-24">24-hour</label>
        </div>
<Code code={`
Expressive.Timepicker.init(document.getElementById('timepicker-24'), {
  twelveHour: false
});
`} />
  </Section>

  <Section {...S.seconds}>
        <p>
          Set <code>showSeconds: true</code> to add a third field after the minutes. It is a spin button named by <code>i18n.seconds</code>. It steps and takes typed values like the minute field, and an emptied field takes the current second. The written value becomes <code>HH:MM:SS</code>, with AM or PM on a 12-hour clock. A value read without seconds, such as <code>14:30</code>, opens at zero seconds.
        </p>
        <p>
          The dial keeps its hours and minutes views, as the Material 3 time picker does. You set seconds in the field only. Under <code>autoSubmit</code>, choosing a minute on the dial writes the time with the seconds the field shows.
        </p>
        <div class="field mb-3">
          <input type="text" class="time-picker no-autoinit" id="timepicker-seconds">
          <label for="timepicker-seconds">With seconds</label>
        </div>
<Code code={`
Expressive.Timepicker.init(document.getElementById('timepicker-seconds'), {
  showSeconds: true
});
`} />
        <p>
          A <code>type="time"</code> input gets 24-hour <code>HH:MM:SS</code>. Give it <code>step="1"</code> so the browser doesn't flag the seconds as a step mismatch.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<input type="time" step="1" class="time-picker" id="alarm">
`} />
  </Section>

  <Section {...S["time-range"]}>
        <p>
          Set <code>minTime</code> and <code>maxTime</code> to bound the times a person can choose. Pass them as 24-hour <code>'HH:MM'</code> or <code>'HH:MM:SS'</code> strings. Either one alone bounds one end of the day. The range can't cross midnight. If <code>minTime</code> is after <code>maxTime</code>, the picker logs a warning and ignores both. Without <code>showSeconds</code>, the picker writes whole minutes, so <code>minTime</code> rounds up and <code>maxTime</code> rounds down to a minute.
        </p>
        <p>
          An hour stays available while any minute of it is in range. With 09:00 to 17:30, the hour 17 is available and its minutes stop at 30. The picker dims dial ticks outside the range, and the hand won't stop on them. Releasing on one changes nothing and fires no <code>select</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the fields, Up and Down stop at the last allowed value instead of wrapping. The picker refuses a typed time outside the range. Each field's <code>aria-valuemin</code> and <code>aria-valuemax</code> follow the allowed values. On a 12-hour clock, the picker disables the AM or PM button when no time in that half of the day is allowed.
        </p>
        <p>
          A value outside the range, in the input or <code>defaultTime</code>, opens at the nearest bound. So does a minute that an hour change leaves outside the range. For example, choosing 17 at 16:45 gives 17:30.
        </p>
        <div class="field mb-3">
          <input type="text" class="time-picker no-autoinit" id="timepicker-range">
          <label for="timepicker-range">Opening hours, 09:00 to 17:30</label>
        </div>
<Code code={`
Expressive.Timepicker.init(document.getElementById('timepicker-range'), {
  minTime: '09:00',
  maxTime: '17:30'
});
`} />
        <p>
          The picker doesn't check a value typed straight into the input. On a <code>type="time"</code> input, also set <code>min</code> and <code>max</code>, so the browser flags a value outside the range.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<input type="time" min="09:00" max="17:30" class="time-picker" id="appointment">
`} />
  </Section>

  <Section {...S.methods}>
        <InstanceNote plugin="Timepicker" />

        <h5 class="light">.showView();</h5>
        <p>Show the hours or minutes face.</p>
        <p><strong>String:</strong> <code>'hours'</code> or <code>'minutes'</code>.</p>
<Code code={`
instance.showView('hours');
`} />

        <h5 class="light">.done();</h5>
        <p>Write the current time to the input, with seconds when <code>showSeconds</code> is on. Pass a truthy argument to clear the input instead.</p>
<Code code={`
instance.done();
instance.done(true);
`} />

        <h5 class="light">.clear();</h5>
        <p>Clear the input. Same as <code>done(true)</code>.</p>
<Code code={`
instance.clear();
`} />

        <h5 class="light">.destroy();</h5>
        <p>Destroy the plugin instance, remove the clock, and tear down its event handlers. The method also cancels pending automatic submission and clock updates, so the destroyed instance cannot write to the input.</p>
<Code code={`
instance.destroy();
`} />
  </Section>

  <Section {...S.properties}>
        <PropertiesTable el="The input the plugin was initialized with.">
            <tr>
              <td><code>time</code></td>
              <td>String</td>
              <td>The last value written by <code>done()</code>, without the AM/PM suffix. It ends in <code>:SS</code> when <code>showSeconds</code> is on.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>hours</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td>The hour currently shown on the clock.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>minutes</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td>The minute currently shown on the clock.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>seconds</code></td>
              <td>Number</td>
              <td>The second of the time on the clock. The seconds field shows it when <code>showSeconds</code> is on.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>amOrPm</code></td>
              <td>String</td>
              <td><code>'AM'</code> or <code>'PM'</code> when <code>twelveHour</code> is true.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>currentView</code></td>
              <td>String</td>
              <td><code>'hours'</code> or <code>'minutes'</code>.</td>
            </tr>
        </PropertiesTable>
  </Section>

</DocsLayout>
